 # Hierarchical Sampling (Inverse CDF Sampling)
 
-Also called: Inverse CDF Sampling, Fine Sampling, Inverse Opacity Sampling
+Also called: Fine Sampling, Hierarchical Importance Sampling, Inverse CDF Sampling
 
 ## Overview
-Hierarchical Sampling (Inverse CDF Sampling) is a method for generating importance samples along a ray in volume rendering by inverting the cumulative distribution function (CDF) of the rendering contribution [S1, S2]. It solves the problem of finding the ray parameter $t$ corresponding to a target cumulative density value $y$, allowing samples to be drawn directly from transmittance estimates rather than computing expensive integrals [S2]. Unlike standard NeRF hierarchical sampling, which interpolates exponentiated densities (probabilities) and requires auxiliary losses, this method interpolates the density field directly (piecewise linear or constant), offering better numerical stability for end-to-end optimization [S1]. It enables Monte Carlo estimates with fewer ray points and allows gradients to back-propagate through the sampling process to the density field parameters [S1].
+Hierarchical Sampling (Inverse CDF Sampling) generates importance samples along a ray in volume rendering by inverting the cumulative distribution function (CDF) of the rendering contribution [S1, S2]. It solves the problem of finding the ray parameter $t$ corresponding to a target cumulative density value $y$, allowing samples to be drawn directly from transmittance estimates rather than computing expensive integrals [S2]. Unlike standard NeRF hierarchical sampling, which interpolates exponentiated densities (probabilities) and requires auxiliary losses, this method interpolates the density field directly (piecewise linear or constant), offering better numerical stability for end-to-end optimization [S1]. It enables Monte Carlo estimates with fewer ray points and allows gradients to back-propagate through the sampling process to the density field parameters [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs:**
-*   `coarse_z_vals`: 1D tensor of depth coordinates for coarse samples, shape `(N_c,)` or `(batch, N_c)`.
-*   `coarse_weights`: 1D tensor of unnormalized importance weights (typically derived from volume density or opacity) corresponding to the coarse samples, shape `(N_c,)` or `(batch, N_c)`.
-*   `N_samples`: Integer specifying the number of fine samples to generate per ray.
-*   `deterministic`: Boolean flag. If `True`, uses deterministic uniform spacing; otherwise uses random jitter.
